UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 35, Lesson 1: Sharing our movement work', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Sharing movement work means performing for an audience and responding respectfully. Performers prepare, begin with a still shape, move with focus and finish with a held pose. Audience members watch quietly, clap kindly and give feedback as two stars and a wish. Performers may choose not to be watched closely: they can perform for one person or share their plan instead.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWrite one star and one wish for a dancer who moved smoothly but looked at the floor the whole time.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStar: smooth, controlled movements. Wish: lift your head and look at the audience. Feedback names a specific strength and one idea to improve.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving audience feedback means judging the dancer. Feedback helps performers improve and should always be kind and specific.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nRehearse and share your sequence or dance with an audience. Record two stars and a wish, make one change and perform again. Write a short reflection on how you felt before and after, and what helped.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Sharing movement work means performing for an audience and responding respectfully. Performers prepare, begin with a still shape, move with focus and finish with a held pose. Audience members watch quietly, clap kindly and give feedback as two stars and a wish. Performers may choose not to be watched closely: they can perform for one person or share their plan instead.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn and discuss","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-MSP-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Rehearse and share your sequence or dance with an audience. Record two stars and a wish, make one change and perform again. Write a short reflection on how you felt before and after, and what helped. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using performing on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Believing audience feedback means judging the dancer.. Correction: Star: smooth, controlled movements.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing audience feedback means judging the dancer. Feedback helps performers improve and should always be kind and specific. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating performing on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with performing, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about performing with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for performing. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for performing. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Star: smooth, controlled movements","Believing audience feedback means judging the dancer","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson"],"question":"Write one star and one wish for a dancer who moved smoothly but looked at the floor the whole time. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for performing; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Sharing movement work means performing for an audience and responding respectfully","Star: smooth, controlled movements","Careful use of an audience and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Believing audience feedback means judging the dancer"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with performing?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named an audience and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply performing to new material and name the an audience and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Believing audience feedback means judging the dancer","Name an audience and explain why it matters only, without linking it to performing"],"question":"Which response best applies performing with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses safe, healthy or respectful choices appropriately for this lesson on performing. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing audience feedback means judging the dancer","Star: smooth, controlled movements","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty"],"question":"Which response best uses safe, healthy or respectful choices for performing?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Believing audience feedback means judging the dancer","Name a body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy and say how it helps with performing"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about performing?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Sharing movement work means performing for an audience and responding) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use a put-down and call it honesty","Example: Lola performs her sequence for her family","Believing audience feedback means judging the dancer","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about performing?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check safety and fairness, include others, and explain the healthier choice","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster"],"question":"Which action keeps safe, healthy or respectful choices accurate for performing?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example","Believing audience feedback means judging the dancer","Name an audience and explain why it matters only, with no linked example","Rehearse and share your sequence or dance with an audience"],"question":"Which independent response best applies performing to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about performing with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating performing on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using performing on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for performing. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with performing, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Believing audience feedback means judging the dancer.. Correction: Star: smooth, controlled movements.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w35-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise performing.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Movement Skill and Performance","success_criteria":["I can use performing with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify an audience and explain why it matters.","I can practise a held pose independently on a new prompt.","I can notice kind feedback needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Sharing movement work means performing for an audience and responding respectfully.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Sharing movement work means performing for an audience and responding respectfully","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about performing with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Lola performs her sequence for her family. She starts with a still pose, counts to herself, performs and holds the finish for 3 seconds. Feedback. Star: smooth links. Star: big, clear shapes. Wish: look at the audience more. She writes one change to make next time.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite one star and one wish for a dancer who moved smoothly but looked at the floor the whole time.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStar: smooth, controlled movements. Wish: lift your head and look at the audience. Feedback names a specific strength and one idea to improve.","explain":"Sharing movement work means performing for an audience and responding respectfully. Performers prepare, begin with a still shape, move with focus and finish with a held pose. Audience members watch quietly, clap kindly and give feedback as two stars and a wish. Performers may choose not to be watched closely: they can perform for one person or share their plan instead.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing audience feedback means judging the dancer. Feedback helps performers improve and should always be kind and specific. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Start and finish a performance.","caption":"Begin and end still.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Start</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">still pose</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Finish</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">held pose, 3 seconds</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Lola performs her sequence for her family. She starts with a still pose, counts to herself, performs and holds  Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Lola performs her sequence for her family. She starts with a still pose, counts to herself, performs and holds  Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or","A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about performing with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Lola performs her sequence for her family. She starts with a still pose, counts to herself, performs and holds the finish for 3 seconds. Feedback. Star: smooth links. Star: big, clear shapes. Wish: look at the audience more. She writes one change to make next time. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"A star and a wish.","caption":"Be kind and specific.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Star</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">smooth links</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Wish</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">look at the audience</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison.","options":["Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about performing with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving audience feedback means judging the dancer. Feedback helps performers improve and should always be kind and specific. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Lola performs her sequence for her family. She starts with a still pose, counts to herself, performs and holds the finish for 3 seconds. Feedback. Star: smooth links. Star: big, clear shapes. Wish: look at the audience more. She writes one change to make next time.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStar: smooth, controlled movements. Wish: lift your head and look at the audience. Feedback names a specific strength and one idea to improve.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Believing audience feedback means judging the dancer. Feedback helps performers improve and should always be kind and specific. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about performing with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSharing movement work means performing for an audience and responding respectfully. Performers prepare, begin with a still shape, move with focus and finish with a held pose. Audience members watch quietly, clap kindly and give feedback as two stars and a wish. Performers may choose not to be watched closely: they can perform for one person or share their plan instead. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 35, Lesson 1: Sharing our movement work","unit_number":7,"unit_title":"Gymnastics and dance","week":35,"week_in_unit":5,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Lola performs her sequence for her family. She starts with a still pose, counts to herself, performs and holds the finish for 3 seconds. Feedback. Star: smooth links. Star: big, clear shapes. Wish: look at the audience more. She writes one change to make next time.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Start and finish a performance.","caption":"Begin and end still.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Start</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">still pose</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Finish</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">held pose, 3 seconds</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"A star and a wish.","caption":"Be kind and specific.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Star</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">smooth links</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Wish</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">look at the audience</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-pdhpe-y3-w35-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
